{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"RAFAEL is an open-domain factoid question answering system for Polish.","source_span":"an open-domain factoid question answering system for Polish, RAFAEL, is presented"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"RAFAEL stands for RApid Factoid Answer Extraction aLgorithm.","source_span":"RAFAEL (RApid Factoid Answer Extraction aLgorithm)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"Deep Entity Recognition (DeepER) is a novel technique introduced and implemented in the paper.","source_span":"a novel technique, called Deep Entity Recognition (DeepER), is introduced and implemented"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"DeepER allows a comprehensive search of all forms of entity references matching a given WordNet synset, based on a previously assembled entity library.","source_span":"It allows a comprehensive search of all forms of entity references matching a given WordNet synset (e.g. an impressionist), based on a previously assembled entity library."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"The entity library was created by analyzing the first sentences of encyclopaedia entries and disambiguation and redirect pages.","source_span":"It has been created by analysing the first sentences of encyclopaedia entries and disambiguation and redirect pages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"DeepER experiments include over a thousand questions from a Polish quiz TV show answered on the grounds of Polish Wikipedia.","source_span":"including over a thousand questions from a quiz TV show answered on the grounds of Polish Wikipedia"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"In the monarch example, Simeon II is the answer because he is a tsar, and WordNet relations imply that a tsar is a monarch.","source_span":"we could utilise information that Simeon II (our answer) is a tsar; thanks to WordNet relations we know that it implies being a monarch"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"The Arctic Tern (Sterna paradisaea) is described in Wikipedia as a seabird of the tern family Sternidae, allowing DeepER to classify it as a bird despite not being a classical NER entity.","source_span":"The Arctic Tern (Sterna paradisaea) is a seabird of the tern family Sternidae."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"With DeepER, a candidate answer can undergo the same recognition process and be compared to the actual expected entity rather than plain string equality.","source_span":"with DeepER, a candidate answer can undergo the same recognition process and be compared to the actual expected entity, not string"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","statement":"The final system evaluation is executed on a separate test set, previously unused in development, and checked manually to avoid overfitting.","source_span":"To avoid overfitting, the final system evaluation is executed on a separate test set, previously unused in development, and is checked manually."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What does RAFAEL stand for and what language does it target?","answer":"RAFAEL stands for RApid Factoid Answer Extraction aLgorithm, an open-domain factoid question answering system for Polish."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"What is Deep Entity Recognition (DeepER) and what does it allow?","answer":"DeepER is a novel entity recognition technique introduced in the paper that allows a comprehensive search of all forms of entity references matching a given WordNet synset, based on a previously assembled entity library."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How was the DeepER entity library created?","answer":"It was created by analysing the first sentences of encyclopaedia entries, along with disambiguation and redirect pages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What test set was used for DeepER's automatic evaluation experiments?","answer":"Over a thousand questions from a Polish quiz TV show, answered on the basis of Polish Wikipedia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","fact_refs":[6],"question":"In the paper's monarch example, what is the answer and how does DeepER connect it to the question about an exiled European monarch?","answer":"The answer is Simeon II. DeepER identifies him as a tsar, and WordNet relations imply that a tsar is a monarch, satisfying the question's constraint."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What example shows DeepER identifying an entity that standard NER would miss?","answer":"The Arctic Tern is not recognized as a named entity by classical NER, but using its Wikipedia definition, DeepER can classify it as a seabird (a type of bird) and include it as a candidate answer."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0043","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Why is the final RAFAEL system evaluation done on a separate test set with manual checking?","answer":"To avoid overfitting, the final evaluation uses a separate test set that was previously unused in development, and the results are checked manually."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","statement":"The paper adds a neural network global lexicon model (NNGLM) and a neural network joint model (NNJM) to a phrase-based SMT system for grammatical error correction.","source_span":"we address these limitations by using two different yet complementary neural network models, namely a neural network global lexicon model and a neural network joint model"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","statement":"Phrase-based SMT systems suffer from limitations of discrete word representation, linear mapping, and lack of global context.","source_span":"phrase-based SMT systems suffer from limitations of discrete word representation, linear mapping, and lack of global context"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","statement":"The paper achieves statistically significant improvement in accuracy for grammatical error correction over a state-of-the-art GEC system.","source_span":"we achieve statistically significant improvement in accuracy for grammatical error correction over a state-of-the-art GEC system"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","statement":"The F0.5 measure, which weights precision twice as much as recall, is the evaluation metric widely used for GEC and was the official metric of the CoNLL 2014 shared task.","source_span":"The INLINEFORM6 measure BIBREF19 , which weights precision twice as much as recall, is the evaluation metric widely used for GEC and was the official evaluation metric adopted in the CoNLL 2014 shared task"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","statement":"Minimum error rate training (MERT) is used to find the optimal feature weights by maximizing the F measure on the development set.","source_span":"To find the optimal feature weights ( INLINEFORM4 ), we use minimum error rate training (MERT), maximizing the INLINEFORM5 measure on the development set"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","statement":"One of the first SMT approaches to GEC focused on correcting countability errors of mass nouns (e.g., many informations -> much information), using an artificially constructed parallel corpus.","source_span":"One of the first approaches of using SMT for GEC focused on correction of countability errors of mass nouns (e.g., many informations INLINEFORM0 much information) BIBREF11 . They had to use an artificially constructed parallel corpus for training their SMT system."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","statement":"The authors state there is no prior work that uses NNGLM and NNJM together for SMT-based grammatical error correction.","source_span":"To the best of our knowledge, there is no prior work that uses these two neural network models for SMT-based GEC."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What two neural network components does the paper add to a phrase-based SMT system for grammatical error correction?","answer":"A neural network global lexicon model (NNGLM) and a neural network joint model (NNJM)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What three limitations of phrase-based SMT systems does the paper aim to address?","answer":"Discrete word representation, linear mapping, and lack of global context."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What evaluation metric was used for grammatical error correction and what does it emphasize?","answer":"The F0.5 measure, which weights precision twice as much as recall; it was the official evaluation metric of the CoNLL 2014 shared task."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","fact_refs":[4],"question":"How are the optimal feature weights found in the phrase-based translation framework used for GEC?","answer":"By using minimum error rate training (MERT) to maximize the F measure on the development set."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What was one of the first uses of SMT for grammatical error correction, and what example did it target?","answer":"Correcting countability errors of mass nouns, such as 'many informations' to 'much information', using an artificially constructed parallel corpus for training."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0044","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What novelty claim does the paper make regarding NNGLM and NNJM for grammatical error correction?","answer":"The authors claim there is no prior work using both the neural network global lexicon model and the neural network joint model together for SMT-based GEC."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","statement":"82% of sentences generated by the LSTM-with-attention model were judged correct in manual annotation, a 10.3% improvement over an LSTM baseline.","source_span":"we found that 82% of generated sentences are correct, an improvement of 10.3% over an LSTM baseline"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","statement":"The entailment generation dataset, filtered from SNLI for entailment-class pairs, has a training set of 183,416 sentence pairs, a development set of 3,329 pairs, and a test set of 3,368 pairs.","source_span":"This results in a training set of $183,416$ sentence pairs, a development set of $3,329$ pairs and a test of $3,368$ pairs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","statement":"Training uses stochastic gradient descent with a mini-batch size of 64 and the ADAM optimizer with a first momentum coefficient of 0.9 and a second momentum coefficient of 0.999.","source_span":"We use stochastic gradient descent with a mini-batch size of 64 and the ADAM optimizer BIBREF14 with a first momentum coefficient of $0.9$ and a second momentum coefficient of $0.999$ ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","statement":"Out-of-vocabulary words, comprising 10.5% of words, are randomly initialized by sampling uniformly from [-sqrt(3), sqrt(3)] and optimized during training.","source_span":"Out-of-vocabulary words ( $10.5\\%$ ) are randomly initialized by sampling values uniformly from $[-\\sqrt{3}, \\sqrt{3}]$ and optimized during training."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","statement":"Gradients are clipped using a norm of 5.0, and training stops after 25 epochs.","source_span":"Furthermore, we clip gradients using a norm of $5.0$ . We stop training after 25 epochs."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","statement":"Using attention yields only a marginally higher BLEU score than without attention (43.1 vs. 42.8).","source_span":"we found that using attention yields only a marginally higher BLEU score (43.1 vs. 42.8)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","statement":"100 random test sentences were manually annotated to decide whether the generated sentence could indeed be inferred from the source sentence.","source_span":"we manually annotated 100 random test sentences and decided whether the generated sentence can indeed be inferred from the source sentence"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What accuracy did the LSTM-with-attention model achieve on manually annotated entailment generation, and how does it compare to the baseline?","answer":"It achieved 82% accuracy, a 10.3% improvement over the LSTM baseline."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","fact_refs":[1],"question":"How large are the training, development, and test sets for the entailment generation task derived from SNLI?","answer":"The training set has 183,416 sentence pairs, the development set has 3,329 pairs, and the test set has 3,368 pairs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What optimizer and hyperparameters were used to train the sequence-to-sequence entailment generation model?","answer":"Stochastic gradient descent with a mini-batch size of 64 and the ADAM optimizer, using a first momentum coefficient of 0.9 and a second momentum coefficient of 0.999."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How were out-of-vocabulary words handled during training of the entailment generation model?","answer":"The 10.5% of words that were out-of-vocabulary were randomly initialized by sampling uniformly from [-√3, √3] and then optimized during training."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What gradient clipping norm and number of training epochs were used?","answer":"Gradients were clipped using a norm of 5.0, and training stopped after 25 epochs."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0045","fact_refs":[5,6],"question":"How did BLEU scores compare with and without attention, and how many test sentences were manually evaluated for correctness?","answer":"BLEU was 43.1 with attention versus 42.8 without attention, and 100 random test sentences were manually annotated to judge correctness."}
